    slug: 'outsourced-handoff-acceptance-criteria',
    title: 'Write Acceptance Criteria for an Outsourced Work Handoff',
    excerpt: 'Turn "take this over" into a reviewable service handoff with explicit inputs, completion evidence, exceptions, and owner decisions.',
    published: '2026-09-03', displayDate: 'September 3, 2026', minutes: 10,
    body: [
      'A handoff to a Philippines-based service specialist should begin with acceptance criteria, not a list of activities. Activities describe motion: check the inbox, update the system, follow up with the requester. Acceptance criteria describe the state that must exist when the work is ready for review. They answer which source was used, which required fields are present, where the result is recorded, what evidence shows completion, and which conditions must be returned to an owner. This distinction gives the specialist a finish line and gives the manager a fair basis for review. Without it, both sides are forced to judge quality from effort, speed, or memory.',
      'Start by choosing one recurring output rather than transferring an entire department. A weekly status packet, a reconciled customer record, or a categorized support queue is narrow enough to observe. Collect three recent examples: one accepted without changes, one corrected during review, and one that could not be completed. Compare the examples to find the facts the owner actually used when deciding whether the work was acceptable. Those facts often differ from the written instruction. Turn the differences into explicit checks, while keeping policy choices and unusual exceptions with the accountable business owner.',
      'Define the input boundary before the output. State the approved place where requests arrive, the minimum information required, and the version of any source document that controls the task. If a request arrives through an informal message, decide whether the specialist should redirect it or create a record in the approved queue. Do not ask them to blend conflicting sources silently. A useful rule says that complete, consistent inputs can proceed; incomplete inputs move to clarification; conflicting approved inputs move to an owner-decision lane. The queue should preserve the original request so the reason for a pause remains visible.',
      'Next, describe completion as evidence in the native system. For a record update, that may be a saved status, linked source, timestamp, and reviewer field. For a report, it may be an approved file name, reporting period, source references, and a recorded review state. Screenshots and duplicate trackers can create privacy and version problems, so use them only when the business has a deliberate reason. The strongest evidence lets another authorized person reproduce the result without reading a long chat transcript or asking the original specialist to remember what happened.',
      'Write rejection conditions alongside acceptance conditions. A task may be unacceptable because a required source is missing, an identifier does not match, a protected field would be exposed, or the requested action exceeds delegated authority. Rejection should not mean blame. It should mean the work moves to a named state with a reason and next owner. Give each condition an ordinary response: request a missing field, quarantine a suspected duplicate, pause a financial action, or escalate a sensitive customer commitment. The specialist needs enough precision to stop safely without deciding the underlying business question.',
      'Separate the review sample from the work queue. During a pilot, the manager can review every item, but that is rarely sustainable. After the routine settles, choose a sampling rule based on risk and change. New task types, new instructions, high-impact fields, and recent defects deserve more attention than stable low-risk work. Record what was sampled and which criterion failed. A correction without a category teaches little. Categorizing source defects, instruction gaps, access failures, execution mistakes, and changed owner decisions helps the team assign the remedy to the right place.',
      'Test the criteria with someone who did not write them. Give that reviewer the source, the output, and the acceptance checklist. If they cannot reach the same decision, the problem may be vague language, missing evidence, or a hidden owner preference. Tighten the criterion around observable facts. Replace "looks professional" with named formatting, content, and approval requirements. Replace "handle urgent items quickly" with defined urgency signals, an approved route, and an owner response expectation. Do not turn judgment-heavy work into false precision; preserve an escalation when reasonable reviewers may disagree.',
      'Access is part of acceptance. The output is not truly ready if it was produced through a shared login, an unapproved export, or permissions broader than the role requires. Map each step to the narrowest practical access and use named accounts wherever the system supports them. Record which owner approves access, when it will be reviewed, and how it will be removed. If the specialist cannot reach a required source, treat that as an access defect instead of encouraging workarounds through copied files or private credentials. A reliable handoff includes a safe path to the work.',
      'Set a change rule before the first revision. Acceptance criteria will evolve as the business sees real exceptions, but an unannounced change can make yesterday’s correct work appear wrong. Keep a version date, short reason, and effective point. Decide whether open items follow the old or new rule. Review a small sample after the change and tell the Philippines-based team which examples are now authoritative. This is especially important when a tool changes fields, a customer promise changes, or an internal owner changes how an exception should be handled.',
      'A practical launch conversation ends with four demonstrations. The specialist shows where a ready item enters, how an ordinary item is completed, how an incomplete item is returned, and how a high-impact exception reaches the owner. The manager then reviews the evidence without verbal coaching. Any missing step becomes a readiness action with a named owner. This exercise is more informative than asking whether everyone understands. It tests the real route, permissions, terminology, and review capacity under conditions close to daily service work.',
      'Good acceptance criteria make outsourced work easier to trust because they keep responsibility visible. The specialist owns consistent execution within the written lane. The manager owns business rules, sensitive decisions, access approval, and changes to the definition of acceptable work. The provider or team lead can coach the routine and surface patterns, but should not invent policy to keep the queue moving. When inputs, finish lines, evidence, rejection states, and decision ownership are explicit, the handoff becomes a manageable service rather than a vague promise to help.'
    ]
